---
// firma2-project-description — the project's OWN description, as its lead
// organization typed it into ProjectFirma. It is the last row of the Key facts
// card in the rail, three lines deep, with the rest a click away.
//
// WHY IT LIVES IN BASICS. It briefly sat on its own band under the project's
// title, on the reasoning that it is the one scalar answering "what IS this
// project" and so earns the page's most valuable inches. It does not. Under the
// title it read as a LEDE — a paragraph explaining the screen — which is the
// one thing design-principles bans there, and it pushed the tracked data (the
// measures, the dollars, the milestones this page exists to answer for) a
// screenful down to do it. It is a SCALAR: a stored fact, correctable, with no
// history worth reading. The rail is where this record's scalars live and the
// Key facts card is the index card, so the description is a row on it — the
// narrative row, beside the lead organization and the program and the county.
//
// LAST ROW, NOT FIRST. The five pairs above it are short and scannable and are
// what a reader uses to PLACE the project; the narrative is what they read if
// they want it, so it comes after. It also puts the disclosure at the bottom
// edge of the card: expanding grows the card downward into empty rail, and
// never shoves a fact the reader was looking at off its line.
//
// IT IS A DATUM, NOT COPY. Worth stating plainly because the section it sits in
// is otherwise all short labelled values: this renders ProjectFirma's
// `ProjectDescription` field, authored by the project's sponsor and editable by
// anyone with permission on the record. Deleting it would delete data.
//
// THE MACHINERY LIVES IN firma2-prose-field now — the measured three-line
// clamp, the Show more toggle, the edit-in-place row — extracted when the
// measure page's Key facts needed the identical shape for Definition and
// Reporter guidance. What stays HERE is the one project-specific rule: a
// sponsor who has not written a description yet is a real state on a real
// record, and an empty three-line box under the title is a worse answer than
// no row at all — the page's remaining sections still say what the project is.
// (The measure rows make the opposite call, with a placeholder, because their
// prose is required before the record can publish.)
import Firma2ProseField from '../shared/firma2-prose-field.astro';

interface Props {
  /**
   * The stored ProjectDescription — two sentences from the lead organization on
   * what the work is and why. Pass `detail.projectDescription`.
   */
  description: string;
}

const { description } = Astro.props;

// Trimmed, because whitespace is not content.
const text = description?.trim() ?? '';
---
{text && (
  <!-- THE LABEL IS VISIBLE HERE, and was not when this sat under the title:
       there, the paragraph's position directly beneath the project's name said
       what it was and the word would have been printed over it for nothing.
       In Key facts it is the seventh row under six labelled ones, so an
       unlabelled block of prose is the odd one out — and "Description" is the
       record's own field name, the same rule the rows above follow (they take
       the Projects table's column headers verbatim). -->
  <Firma2ProseField field="projectDescription" label="Description" value={text} />
)}
